00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.470 align:start position:0%
 
my name is max pemberton i'm a doctor  and a journalist  and until a few years ago i was also a  very heavy smoker  while i didn't have great problems in  stopping smoking myself  i know that from my clinical practice  some people really struggle with  quitting  what's interesting is that with the  emergence of e-cigarettes  people are taking control of their  cigarette use in a really different way  about half of the people that use  e-cigarettes are using them to quit  entirely but the other half are using  them  long-term with no intention of quitting  at all  e-cigarette users argue that they enjoy  using it and that there's no  harm from long-term nicotine use so  should i be promoting this as an  alternative to smoking  i'm going to speak to professor claire  geraider former head of the royal  college of general practitioners  and a gp in south london with a special  interest in addiction  there are some people that will say you  know if you're addicted to nicotine so  what doesn't matter  you know nicotine is a harmless  substance it's not going to be  doing any damage to their health so what  does it really matter if they're  addicted  80 percent of people who have more than  two cigarettes  will be lifelong smokers so it is an  incredibly  addictive drug far more addictive than  heroin  far more addictive than many of the  other substances that young people will  take  this weekend i am abstinent from the  behavior of smoking  i do not have to worry about the end of  a meal  where can i have my e-cigarette i don't  have to worry at the end of a long  flight  oh gosh i can now have my e-cigarette i  am free  from all of that addictive behavior that  is a far better place to be  than carrying around an e-cigarette and  always having that cognition  that addictive mind i must get off this  plane  quickly i must get out of the restaurant  can i smoke an  e-cigarette so it is always better in my  mind as a doctor  to not medicalize i.e prescribe  e-cigarettes buy them  but rather to remove the behavior  claire takes a fairly hard-line medical  perspective  her view is that all addiction is bad  and that these people are still addicted  to nicotine while i understand her point  of view  i worry that we're losing sight of the  fact that  actually nicotine products save lives  as a doctor that's something i'm far  more interested in  than whether or not somebody's  technically an addict i wanted to get a  different  medical perspective so i'm going to  speak to dr roger  henderson he's a gp based in shropshire  and he's also a regular contributor to  spectator health  he's got a special interest in smoking  cessation and has been the face of  several nhs  quit campaigns roger as doctors should  we be  promoting abstinence as the answer to  nicotine addiction  in an ideal world yes the trouble is we  don't live in an ideal world and the  days when you had  smoker who smoked bad smoker who quit  good  have really gone and i think tobacco  risk reduction  tobacco harm reduction is now the  buzzword and so smokers that either  cannot quit  or choose not to quit should have access  to treatments  to maintain them away from their smoke  and smoking cigarettes  and if that means they remain on  nicotine replacement for a while or even  long term  personally i'm comfortable with that if  people want to use  nicotine replacement therapy in the long  term do you have a problem with that  i'm completely relaxed about that and  the reason is this  because it is not nicotine that kills  and maims our smokers  it is smoke what i want is for our  smokers to quit  smoke i don't really care if they don't  quit nicotine ideally they should  but if they're not smoking if they're  not putting smoke into their lungs  they're not harming themselves nicotine  essentially is no more  harmful to me or to you than that cup of  coffee we've just had  okay that's an important point and  people slightly get confused when they  think about nicotine they think nicotine  is harmful it is not  smoke kills our smokers not nicotine  roger takes a very pragmatic view of  nicotine addiction  for him from a harm reduction point of  view the development of things like  e-cigarettes has been a massive step in  the right direction  module is clear it's not nicotine that  kills people  it's smoking so smoking is what we  should all be focusing on  rather than tying ourselves up in knots  around whether or not somebody is  addicted to nicotine  for him abstinence is not the only way  what about the psychological aspects of  smoking there's no doubt  for lots of people cigarettes form a  really important  habitual aspect to their lives and also  is there any positive aspects of  nicotine does it have  any effects on the body at all or is it  an inert substance  professor dinesh bugra is the former  president of the royal college of  psychiatrists  and the current president of the world  psychiatric association  and he explains people who use nicotine  will say  you know it helps them with their  concentration helps them with you know  feeling relaxed and things like that  so are there any benefits to using  nicotine i think it's exactly that that  you know people do feel calm people do  feel  that they can concentrate better and  it's also you know the first  hit of the day which can you know gets  them going  but the point is not what it is  doing in the sense that you know the  kind of positive  aspects it's the addictive nature  and it's almost you know building on  more and more more you smoke more you  need and more you need  more you smoke so it just kind of builds  into  a long-standing long-term addiction  problem  although nicotine might not be  responsible for the deaths that we see  with  cigarette smoking that actually nicotine  does still have an effect on the brain  in some ways so it's not a completely  inert substance no it's not an inert  substance i mean it is a chemical  substance which affects  brain cells so i think that's something  that we need to  take on board and take it seriously what  is the impact  of using nicotine long term i think that  certainly there has been considerable  amount of evidence suggesting that it  affects people's  mental health and we know that people  who have mental illness uh  are more likely to use nicotine because  it kind of you know gets rid  of hallucinations and delusions and  makes them feel better but it also means  that it interferes with the medication  that they're on  and we also know that you know patients  bidsky disorders are likely to die 15 to  20 years younger than those who do not  so i think there are those kind of  long-term issues that we need to be  aware of and we need to keep an eye and  watch out for  there's no doubt that for many people  who use e-cigarettes or nicotine-based  products  that for them this is a civil liberties  issue they argue that they're not  hurting anyone  in fact there's no evidence that they're  even hurting themselves so why can't  they keep using these products  i've come to speak to rory broomfield  he's director of the freedom association  and i'm going to ask him to put forward  the civil liberties case  people need to be able to make the  choice over how they  live their lives and how they consume  products whether they be cigarettes  e-cigarettes or other therapies so the  point is  here that e-cigarettes provide the  choice for people to  either consume nicotine in that way or  indeed any other way how do you feel  though  about people using these products long  term  because obviously we're aware that some  people are using them to wean themselves  off nicotine but some people are making  a very active choice to continue using  them  with with no intention of stopping in  the shorter intermediate term  well of course there's an element of  education about  what they are consuming and i think the  medical profession  is trying to provide that information  but  ultimately it's about the individual  choosing whether or not he or she wishes  to  consume or buy an e-cigarette and indeed  the benefits of buying an e-cigarette  opposed to buying a cigarette i think  are well established  finally i'm going to speak to maura  gilchrist director of scientific  engagement at philip morris  i want to ask her about the company's  current research and development  activities  but also whether or not the general  public should really trust tobacco  companies to make an alternative  to the traditional cigarette what's the  focus  of your research and development  activities  well the focus of our research and  development activities  are really to develop to assess and  ultimately to commercialize  a portfolio of products that we believe  have the potential to reduce  smokers health risk and reduce the  health risk to the population overall  we're working on a range of different  products  some that contain tobacco and some that  don't but it's really the combustion of  tobacco  that causes the health-related issues  with cigarettes  it's that high temperature of burning  that causes the generation of the vast  majority  of the chemicals that appear in  cigarette smoke  and particularly the ones that are most  closely associated with  smoking-related diseases why are you a  tobacco company  looking to develop these safer  alternatives not only are we trying to  develop these products but it's our  ambition  ultimately they will completely replace  cigarettes  and people may question why would we be  doing that and make question what our  motives are  but we're doing this to respond to the  needs of smokers  and also ultimately to respond to the  serious concerns that  are known about smoking isn't it just as  bad for somebody to be  addicted to nicotine as it is for them  to just continue smoking  there are certain misconceptions about  nicotine but many public health bodies  including public health england from  here in the uk  they recognize that nicotine is one of  the reasons why smokers  use cigarettes so nicotine is addictive  it's not risk-free but they also  recognize that it's not the primary  cause of smoking-related diseases  if i'm honest many people don't trust  you  are these products not just an attempt  for you to keep your business  despite all the efforts they've been to  try and curb smoking  we understand that some will question  our motives why would we try and  transform what is already  a very profitable business for us  but ultimately this is what smokers are  demanding potentially reduced risk  products so it makes sense  for us as a business it makes sense for  our shareholders  and ultimately it's also completely  aligned with the goals of public health  when would you be in a position to make  a claim that your products  are reduced risk well the exact timing  of  a claim is difficult to predict our  scientific studies are well underway and  the results are encouraging  in fact we're so encouraged that we will  be submitting to the united states food  and drug administration through their  modified risk tobacco product  application process  and we'll be doing that towards the end  of this year  certainly i understand claire's argument  that being addicted to something  isn't nice but as roger alludes to  people choose to use nicotine based  products for a variety of reasons  and really as doctors it's our job to  direct them towards the safest way of  doing this  the stark fact is that smoking kills  half of the people  that smoke anything we can do that moves  people away from traditional cigarettes  has got to be a good thing  smoking kills people nicotine doesn't  for me abstinence is not the only way

